Short:        Bash-compatible shell for AmigaOS (no ixemul)
Author:       AmiBash contributors
Uploader:     farsonic@gmail.com
Type:         util/shell
Version:      0.9.3
Architecture: m68k-amigaos >= 2.0.4; m68k-aros
Distribution: Aminet
License:      GPL-3.0-or-later

AmiBash gives the Amiga the bash experience: bash syntax and scripting,
emacs-style line editing, history with Ctrl-R, tab completion of commands,
files and volumes, functions, arrays, [[ ]], $(( )), here-docs, and the
usual Unix tools built in (ls, cat, grep, sed, sort, find, cut, tr, wc, ...).

It is a native dos.library program: no ixemul.library, no Unix emulation.
Amiga paths (DH0:Work/x) and Unix-style paths (/dh0/Work/x) both work; "/"
lists all volumes and assigns.  Amiga commands run directly with correct
ReadArgs() quoting, Ctrl-C works, return codes become $?.

Double-click it on Workbench (or type "bash --term") for the AmiBash
Terminal: a window with tabs, each running its own bash.  Amiga-T or the
"+" opens a tab, Amiga-W or a tab's "x" closes it, Amiga-1..9 switches.
Amiga-+/- zoom the text: the supplied AmiBashMono font (DejaVu Sans Mono
drawn for every size from 10 to 43 pixels) stays sharp at every step.
On RTG screens it uses an installed monospaced TrueType font, which
AAText (Aminet util/wb/AAText, OS 3.2) draws anti-aliased; install the
included DejaVuSansMono.ttf with AATextManager or, on systems
without ReAction, with the included InstallTTF Shell tool
("InstallTTF Fonts/DejaVuSansMono.ttf COPY").  AGA/ECS screens keep
the AmiBashMono bitmap fonts.
The program is pure (re-entrant), so the tabs share one copy of the code,
and it can be made resident.

Its behaviour is checked against GNU bash 5.2 with a test corpus, and it
runs on 68000 with 1 MB (bash) or 68020+ (bash020).

Install:  double-click Install-AmiBash (Amiga Installer), or by hand:
          Copy bash TO C:        (or bash020 AS C:bash on 68020+)
          Copy S/bashrc TO S:    (optional)
          Copy Fonts/#? TO FONTS: ALL   (terminal font)
          then type "bash" in a Shell window, or "bash --term"
          (Workbench: copy bash and bash.info to a drawer).

See README.md for details, the list of differences from GNU bash and
build instructions (vbcc, NDK 3.2).
